TAO_1 :: #string END
The Tao that can be talked about
is not the Tao that endures.
The name that can be spoken
is not the name that endures.

Nameless, it is where heaven and earth begin.
Named, it is the mother of all things.

So, always free of wanting,
you see its wonder.
Always wanting,
you see only its edges.

The two come out together
and are named apart.
Both are called the mystery.
Mystery beyond mystery,
the gate of every wonder.
END;

TAO_2 :: #string END
When everyone knows beauty as beauty,
there is already ugliness.
When everyone knows the good as good,
there is already what is not good.

So being and nothing give birth to each other.
Hard and easy complete each other.
Long and short measure each other.
High and low lean on each other.
Tone and voice tune each other.
Before and after follow each other.

So the sage does the work of not-doing
and teaches without words.
All things rise, and the sage turns none away,
gives them life and does not own them,
does the work and asks nothing for it,
finishes and does not settle in it.
Because the sage does not settle in it,
it never goes away.
END;

TAO_3 :: #string END
Do not honor the gifted,
and people will not quarrel.
Do not prize rare goods,
and people will not steal.
Do not show what stirs desire,
and people’s hearts will stay calm.

So when the sage governs,
hearts are emptied
and bellies filled,
wills are softened
and bones made strong.
The people are kept free of cunning and craving,
and the clever do not dare to act.

Do the not-doing,
and all will be well ordered.
END;

TAO_4 :: #string END
The Tao is an empty bowl:
use it, and it is never filled.
Deep as a dark pool,
it seems the ancestor of all things.

It wears down the sharp edges,
unravels the tangles,
mutes the light,
joins the dust.
Clear and still as water,
it seems perhaps to be there.

I do not know whose child it is.
It seems to have come before the first of the gods.
END;

TAO_5 :: #string END
Heaven and earth are not tender-hearted;
they treat all things as straw dogs.
The sage is not tender-hearted
and treats the people as straw dogs.

The space between heaven and earth —
is it not like a bellows?
Empty, it never gives out.
Worked, it breathes out more and more.

Many words soon run dry.
Better to keep to what is within.
END;

TAO_6 :: #string END
The valley spirit never dies.
It is called the hidden mother.
The gate of the hidden mother
is the root of heaven and earth.

Unbroken as a silk thread,
it seems hardly there.
Draw on it,
and it never wears out.
END;

TAO_7 :: #string END
Heaven lasts long, and earth endures.
Why can heaven and earth last so long?
Because they do not live for themselves,
they can go on living.

So the sage puts self last
and is found first,
holds self loosely
and is kept safe.
Is it not by wanting nothing for the self
that the self comes into its own?
END;

TAO_8 :: #string END
Goodness at its highest is like water.
Water does good to all things
and quarrels with none.
It settles where no one wants to be,
and so it is close to the Tao.

A good house stands on good ground.
A good heart is deep.
Good giving is kind.
Good words are true.
Good rule brings order.
Good work is skilled.
Good action comes in its time.

Because it does not quarrel,
no one finds fault with it.
END;

TAO_9 :: #string END
Filling a cup and holding it full —
better to have stopped in time.
Hammer a blade to its finest edge,
and the edge will not last.
A hall full of gold and jade
can never be kept safe.
Wealth and rank worn with pride
bring their own ruin.

When the work is done,
go quietly away.
This is the way of heaven.
END;

TAO_10 :: #string END
Carrying body and soul and holding to the One,
can you keep them from coming apart?
Gathering your breath until it is soft,
can you be like a newborn?
Wiping clean the dark mirror,
can you leave it without a spot?
Loving the people and ruling the land,
can you do it without cleverness?
When heaven’s gates open and close,
can you be like the mother bird?
Seeing clearly in every direction,
can you do without doing?

Give life and nourish it.
Give life, and do not own it.
Do the work, and ask nothing for it.
Raise, and do not rule.
This is called the deep virtue.
END;

TAO_11 :: #string END
Thirty spokes meet at one hub;
in its emptiness
lies the use of the cart.

Clay is kneaded into a bowl;
in its emptiness
lies the use of the bowl.

Doors and windows are cut into a room;
in its emptiness
lies the use of the room.

So the solid gives us the benefit,
and the empty gives us the use.
END;

TAO_12 :: #string END
The five colors blind the eye.
The five notes deafen the ear.
The five flavors dull the tongue.
Racing and hunting drive the heart wild.
Rare goods lead people astray.

So the sage feeds the belly, not the eye,
letting go of that
and taking this.
END;

TAO_13 :: #string END
Favor and disgrace alike are startling.
We hold on to great trouble
as we hold on to the body.

What does it mean, favor and disgrace alike are startling?
Favor makes us small.
We are startled when we get it,
startled when we lose it.
That is what it means.

What does it mean, we hold on to great trouble
as we hold on to the body?
I have great trouble
because I have a body.
If I had no body,
what trouble could I have?

So whoever values the world
as much as their own body
can be trusted with the world.
Whoever cares for the world
as gently as for their own body
can be given the world to keep.
END;

TAO_14 :: #string END
Looked at, it is not seen:
call it the hidden.
Listened for, it is not heard:
call it the silent.
Reached for, it is not touched:
call it the fine.
These three cannot be searched out,
so they blend into one.

Its upper side does not shine.
Its lower side is not in shadow.
On and on like a thread,
it cannot be named,
and it goes back to nothing.
This is the shape of the shapeless,
the image of what is not,
blurred and fleeting.

Meet it, and you see no face.
Follow it, and you see no back.
Hold to the Tao of old
to steer the things of now.
To know the ancient beginning:
this is the thread of the Tao.
END;

TAO_15 :: #string END
The skilled ones of old
were subtle, wondrous, dark and open,
too deep to be known.
Because they cannot be known,
I can only try to say how they seemed.

Hesitant, as if wading a river in winter.
Wary, as if afraid of neighbors on every side.
Grave, like a guest in someone’s house.
Loosening, like ice about to melt.
Plain, like uncarved wood.
Wide, like a valley.
Murky, like muddy water.

Who can be muddy
and, by keeping still,
slowly grow clear?
Who can be at rest
and, by moving long,
slowly come to life?

Whoever keeps this Tao
does not want to be full.
Never full,
they can wear out
and need no making new.
END;

TAO_16 :: #string END
Reach all the way to emptiness.
Hold stillness steady.
All things rise together,
and I watch them turn back.
Things grow thick and green,
and each goes home to its root.

Going home to the root is stillness.
Stillness is returning to the life one was given.
Returning to that life is the constant.
Knowing the constant is clear sight.
Not knowing the constant,
people act blindly and come to harm.

Knowing the constant, you have room for all.
Having room for all, you are fair.
Being fair, you are whole.
Being whole, you are like heaven.
Being like heaven, you are of the Tao.
Being of the Tao, you endure,
and to the end of your days
no harm can find you.
END;

TAO_17 :: #string END
The best rulers:
the people only know that they are there.
Next come those the people love and praise.
Next, those they fear.
Last, those they mock.

Where trust falls short,
there will be no trust.

Unhurried, they are sparing with words.
When the work is done and all is well,
the people say,
“This is just the way we are.”
END;

TAO_18 :: #string END
When the great Tao falls away,
kindness and justice appear.
When cleverness and know-how come,
great pretending follows.
When the family is at odds,
we hear of dutiful children and loving parents.
When the country is dark with disorder,
we hear of loyal ministers.
END;

TAO_19 :: #string END
Cut off saintliness, cast off cleverness,
and the people gain a hundredfold.
Cut off kindness, cast off justice,
and the people go back to loving their parents and children.
Cut off craft, cast off profit,
and thieves and robbers disappear.

These three are only surface
and not enough on their own.
So give the people something to hold to:
look to the undyed silk,
hold the uncarved block,
think less of self,
want less.
END;

TAO_20 :: #string END
Put away your studies,
and your worries go with them.
A polite yes and a careless yes —
how far apart are they?
Good and bad —
how far apart are they?
What others fear,
I must fear too.
But how wide it is,
and never ending!

Everyone is merry,
as if at a great feast,
as if climbing a terrace in spring.
I alone am quiet and give no sign,
like a baby who has not yet smiled,
weary, as if I had no home to go back to.
Everyone has more than enough;
I alone seem left with nothing.
Mine is a fool’s heart,
murky and slow.

Others see everything clearly;
I alone am in the dusk.
Others pick everything apart;
I alone am muddled and dull.
Calm as the sea,
restless as a wind that never stops.
Everyone has some use;
I alone am stubborn and coarse.
I alone am different from others:
I treasure being fed by the mother.
END;

TAO_21 :: #string END
Great virtue, in all its ways,
follows the Tao alone.
The Tao, as a thing,
is shadowy and dim.
Dim and shadowy,
yet there are images in it.
Shadowy and dim,
yet there are things in it.
Hidden and dark,
yet there is a seed in it.
The seed is very real,
and in it is something you can trust.

From now back to the oldest days
its name has never left us,
and through it we see the first of all things.
How do I know how all things begin?
Through this.
END;

TAO_22 :: #string END
What bends is kept whole.
What is crooked comes straight.
What is hollow fills.
What is worn becomes new.
Have little, and you gain.
Have much, and you lose your way.

So the sage holds to the One
and becomes a pattern for the world.
Not showing off, the sage is seen clearly.
Not claiming to be right, the sage shines.
Not boasting, the sage’s work is honored.
Not swelling with pride, the sage endures.
Because the sage does not contend,
no one in the world can contend with the sage.

The old ones said, “What bends is kept whole.”
Was that idle talk?
Become truly whole,
and all things come home to you.
END;

TAO_23 :: #string END
To speak little is natural.
A whirlwind does not last the morning.
A cloudburst does not last the day.
Who makes them? Heaven and earth.
If even heaven and earth cannot keep them going,
how much less can we?

So those who follow the Tao
become one with the Tao.
Those who follow virtue
become one with virtue.
Those who follow loss
become one with loss.

One with the Tao,
the Tao is glad to have you.
One with virtue,
virtue is glad to have you.
One with loss,
loss is glad to have you.

Where trust falls short,
there will be no trust.
END;

TAO_24 :: #string END
On tiptoe, you cannot stand for long.
Taking giant strides, you cannot walk far.
Those who show themselves off are not seen clearly.
Those who insist they are right do not shine.
Those who boast have nothing to show for it.
Those who praise themselves do not last.

To the Tao, these are
scraps of leftover food
and lumps on the body.
Everything seems to dislike them,
so those who have the Tao
do not live this way.
END;

TAO_25 :: #string END
There is something blended and whole,
born before heaven and earth.
Silent, empty,
it stands alone and does not change.
It goes round everywhere and never tires.
It could be the mother of the world.

I do not know its name.
I call it Tao.
Pressed to name it further, I say: great.
Great, it flows on.
Flowing on, it goes far.
Going far, it comes back.

So the Tao is great,
heaven is great,
earth is great,
and the king is great too.
In the world there are four great things,
and the king is one of them.

People take their way from the earth,
earth from heaven,
heaven from the Tao,
and the Tao from what is so of itself.
END;

TAO_26 :: #string END
Heavy is the root of light.
Stillness is lord of the restless.

So the sage, walking all day,
keeps close to the heavy wagon.
There may be grand sights to see,
yet the sage rests at ease, above it all.

How can a lord of ten thousand chariots
carry himself so lightly before the world?
Lightness loses the root.
Restlessness loses the throne.
END;

TAO_27 :: #string END
A good walker leaves no tracks.
A good speaker leaves no slip to pick at.
A good counter needs no counting sticks.
A good door has no bolt,
yet no one can open it.
A good knot has no rope,
yet no one can untie it.

So the sage is always good at saving people
and casts no one away,
always good at saving things
and throws nothing away.
This is called wearing the light.

So the good are teachers to the not-good,
and the not-good are the clay the good work with.
Not to honor the teacher,
not to love the clay —
however clever you are, you are badly lost.
This is the heart of the mystery.
END;

TAO_28 :: #string END
Know the male,
hold to the female,
and be the world’s ravine.
Be the world’s ravine,
and the constant virtue will not leave you.
You come home to being a newborn.

Know the white,
hold to the black,
and be the world’s pattern.
Be the world’s pattern,
and the constant virtue will not fail you.
You come home to the boundless.

Know glory,
hold to the lowly place,
and be the world’s valley.
Be the world’s valley,
and the constant virtue will be enough.
You come home to the uncarved block.

When the block is split, it becomes tools.
In the sage’s hands, they become officers and leaders.
So the finest carving cuts nothing away.
END;

TAO_29 :: #string END
Whoever would take the world
and work on it —
I see they cannot succeed.
The world is a sacred vessel;
it cannot be worked on.
Whoever forces it, spoils it.
Whoever grabs it, loses it.

So among things,
some lead and some follow,
some breathe softly and some blow hard,
some are strong and some are frail,
some stand firm and some come crashing down.

So the sage lets go of the extreme,
the extravagant,
the excessive.
END;

TAO_30 :: #string END
Whoever helps a ruler by the Tao
does not use soldiers to force the world.
Force has a way of coming back.

Where armies camp,
thorns and brambles grow.
After a great army passes,
hungry years are sure to follow.

A good leader does what is needed and stops,
never daring to take by strength.
Do it, but without pride.
Do it, but without boasting.
Do it, but without arrogance.
Do it because there is no other way.
Do it, but do not force.

Whatever reaches its peak grows old.
This is called going against the Tao,
and what goes against the Tao
ends early.
END;

TAO_31 :: #string END
Even fine weapons are tools of ill omen.
All things seem to hate them,
so whoever has the Tao
will not live by them.

At home, the noble honor the left;
at war, they honor the right.
Weapons are tools of ill omen,
not tools for the noble.
When there is no other way, they use them,
holding calm and restraint above all.
Victory is not beautiful.
To find it beautiful
is to take joy in killing,
and whoever takes joy in killing
will never have their way in the world.

In good times the left is honored;
in times of grief, the right.
The second general stands on the left,
the first general on the right:
they are placed as they would be for mourning.
When many are killed,
weep for them with grief and sorrow.
When a war is won,
keep it with the rites of mourning.
END;

TAO_32 :: #string END
The Tao is forever nameless.
Like the uncarved block, it is small,
yet nothing in the world can make it serve.
If lords and kings could hold to it,
all things would come to them as guests.

Heaven and earth would meet
and let fall a sweet dew;
no one would command it,
yet it would settle evenly on all.

Once the block is cut, there are names.
And once there are names,
know where to stop.
To know where to stop
is to be safe.

The Tao in the world
is like brooks in the valleys
running to the rivers and the sea.
END;

TAO_33 :: #string END
To know others is to be clever.
To know yourself is to see clearly.
Winning over others takes muscle.
Winning over yourself takes strength.
To know what is enough is to be rich.
To keep on walking is to have a will.
Not to lose your place is to last.
To die and not be lost
is to live long.
END;

TAO_34 :: #string END
The great Tao overflows;
it can go left, it can go right.
All things depend on it for life,
and it turns none away.
It finishes its work
and claims no name for it.
It clothes and feeds all things
and does not act as their lord.

Always without wanting,
it can be called small.
All things come home to it,
and it does not act as their lord;
it can be called great.
Because it never makes itself great,
it is able to be great.
END;

TAO_35 :: #string END
Hold fast to the great image,
and the whole world comes to you,
comes and meets no harm,
only rest, and peace, and ease.

Music and sweet cakes
will stop a passing traveler.
But the Tao, put into words,
is plain as water, with no taste at all.
Look at it: there is not enough to see.
Listen: there is not enough to hear.
Use it: it can never be used up.
END;

TAO_36 :: #string END
What is about to shrink
must first be stretched.
What is about to weaken
must first be made strong.
What is about to fall
must first be raised up.
What is about to be taken
must first be given.
This is called the subtle light.

The soft and weak overcome the hard and strong.

Fish should not leave the deep water.
A country’s sharpest tools
should not be shown to the people.
END;

TAO_37 :: #string END
The Tao never acts,
and yet there is nothing it does not do.

If lords and kings could hold to it,
all things would change of themselves.
If, as they change, desire should stir,
I would quiet it
with the nameless uncarved block.
The nameless uncarved block
brings freedom from wanting.
Not wanting, and still,
the world would settle of itself.
END;

TAO_38 :: #string END
The highest virtue does not cling to virtue,
and so it has virtue.
Lesser virtue never lets go of virtue,
and so it has none.

The highest virtue does nothing
and needs no reason.
Lesser virtue is busy
and always has its reasons.
The highest kindness acts
but needs no reason.
The highest justice acts
and always has its reasons.
The highest courtesy acts,
and when no one answers,
it bares its arms and hauls them into line.

So when the Tao is lost, there is virtue.
When virtue is lost, there is kindness.
When kindness is lost, there is justice.
When justice is lost, there is ceremony.
Ceremony is loyalty and trust worn thin,
and the first sign of disorder.
Knowing ahead of time
is the Tao’s bright flower
and the start of folly.

So the full-grown person
stays with the thick, not the thin,
with the fruit, not the flower,
letting go of that
and taking this.
END;

TAO_39 :: #string END
In the old days, these found the One.
Heaven found the One and became clear.
Earth found the One and became calm.
The spirits found the One and came alive.
The valley found the One and was filled.
All things found the One and were born.
Lords and kings found the One
and set the world right.
All this is the work of the One.

If heaven were not clear,
it might split.
If earth were not calm,
it might shake apart.
If the spirits were not alive,
they might fade away.
If the valley were not filled,
it might run dry.
If all things were not living,
they might die out.
If lords and kings were not honored and high,
they might stumble and fall.

So the noble grow from a humble root,
and the high stands on the low.
That is why lords and kings call themselves
the orphaned, the lonely, the unworthy.
Is this not rooting the noble in the humble?
Is it not?
Count the parts of a cart,
and there is no cart.
Do not wish to gleam like jade.
Be rough as a stone.
END;

TAO_40 :: #string END
Turning back is how the Tao moves.
Softness is how the Tao works.

All things under heaven come from what is,
and what is comes from what is not.
END;

TAO_41 :: #string END
When the best students hear of the Tao,
they practice it with all their strength.
When middling students hear of it,
they keep it one day and lose it the next.
When the poorest students hear of it,
they roar with laughter.
If it were not laughed at,
it would not be worth calling the Tao.

So the old sayings go:
The bright path looks dark.
The path forward looks like going back.
The level path looks rough.
The highest virtue looks like a valley.
The purest white looks soiled.
Broad virtue looks as if it falls short.
Firm virtue looks idle.
What is truly real looks changeable.
The great square has no corners.
The great vessel is finished last.
The great music is barely heard.
The great image has no shape.

The Tao is hidden and has no name.
Yet only the Tao
gives itself freely and brings all things to completion.
END;

TAO_42 :: #string END
The Tao bears one.
One bears two.
Two bear three.
Three bear all things.

All things carry yin on their backs
and hold yang in their arms,
and blending their breath,
they come into harmony.

What people hate most
is to be orphaned, lonely, unworthy,
yet these are the names that kings and lords call themselves.
So things may gain by losing
and lose by gaining.

What others teach, I teach too:
“The hard and brutal do not die a natural death.”
I will take this as the father of my teaching.
END;

TAO_43 :: #string END
The softest thing in the world
gallops over the hardest.
What is not there at all
slips in where there is no crack.
By this I know the good of not-doing.

Teaching without words,
the good of not-doing —
few in the world ever reach them.
END;

TAO_44 :: #string END
Your name or your life:
which is dearer?
Your life or your goods:
which is worth more?
Gaining or losing:
which is the real sickness?

So much craving costs dearly,
and a full storehouse ends in heavy loss.
Know what is enough, and you will not be shamed.
Know where to stop, and you will not be in danger.
This is how to last a long time.
END;

TAO_45 :: #string END
What is most complete seems to lack something,
yet use never wears it out.
What is most full seems empty,
yet use never drains it.
The straightest seems bent.
The greatest skill seems clumsy.
The greatest eloquence seems tongue-tied.

Moving overcomes cold.
Stillness overcomes heat.
Clear and still,
you set the world right.
END;

TAO_46 :: #string END
When the world has the Tao,
the swift horses are sent back
to carry manure to the fields.
When the world has lost the Tao,
warhorses foal in the borderlands.

There is no disaster like not knowing what is enough,
no fault like the wish to get.
So the enough that comes from knowing enough
is always enough.
END;

TAO_47 :: #string END
You can know the world
without stepping outside.
You can see the way of heaven
without looking through the window.
The farther you wander,
the less you know.

So the sage knows without setting out,
names without looking,
finishes without doing.
END;

TAO_48 :: #string END
Study, and each day there is more.
Follow the Tao, and each day there is less.
Less, and still less,
until you come to not-doing.
Do nothing,
and there is nothing that does not get done.

Win the world
by having no business with it.
Get busy with it,
and you will never win it.
END;

TAO_49 :: #string END
The sage has no fixed heart;
the people’s heart becomes the sage’s heart.

To the good I am good.
To those who are not good
I am good as well,
and so goodness is gained.
With the faithful I keep faith.
With the faithless
I keep faith as well,
and so faith is gained.

The sage lives in the world quietly, drawn in,
and for the world’s sake keeps the heart murky and whole.
The people strain their eyes and ears toward the sage,
and the sage treats them all as little children.
END;

TAO_50 :: #string END
We come out into life
and go back into death.
Three in ten walk with life.
Three in ten walk with death.
And three in ten, just by living,
move on into the place of death.
Why is this?
Because they hold on to life too hard.

I have heard that those who know how to care for life
can walk the hills and meet no rhinoceros or tiger,
can go into battle and need no armor.
The rhinoceros finds no place for its horn,
the tiger no place for its claws,
the blade no place to enter.
Why is this?
Because in them there is no place for death.
END;

TAO_51 :: #string END
The Tao gives them life.
Virtue feeds them.
Matter gives them shape.
Circumstance makes them complete.
So all things honor the Tao
and treasure virtue.
No one commands this honor and this treasuring;
it is always so, of itself.

So the Tao gives them life,
and virtue feeds them,
raises them and rears them,
brings them to fruit and to fullness,
covers them and shelters them.
It gives life and does not own it,
does the work and asks nothing for it,
raises and does not rule.
This is called the deep virtue.
END;

TAO_52 :: #string END
The world has a beginning,
and it is the mother of the world.
Find the mother,
and you know the children.
Know the children,
and go back to keep the mother,
and to the end of your days
no harm can find you.

Block the openings,
shut the doors,
and all your life you will not wear out.
Open them wide,
busy yourself with affairs,
and all your life there is no saving you.

Seeing what is small is clear sight.
Keeping to what is soft is strength.
Use the light,
come back to its brightness,
and bring no harm upon yourself.
This is called learning the constant.
END;

TAO_53 :: #string END
If I had even a grain of sense,
I would walk the great Tao
and fear only wandering off it.
The great Tao is smooth and level,
but people love the byways.

The palace is spotless,
the fields are choked with weeds,
the granaries stand empty.
They wear bright silks,
carry sharp swords at the hip,
gorge on food and drink,
and have more riches than they can use.
This is called the bragging of thieves.
Surely this is not the Tao!
END;

TAO_54 :: #string END
What is well set in the ground
cannot be pulled out.
What is held well
cannot slip away.
Children’s children will keep the offerings
and never let them lapse.

Tend it in yourself,
and your virtue becomes real.
Tend it in your home,
and your virtue overflows.
Tend it in your village,
and your virtue lasts.
Tend it in your country,
and your virtue grows rich.
Tend it in the world,
and your virtue is everywhere.

So see a person through a person,
a home through a home,
a village through a village,
a country through a country,
the world through the world.
How do I know the world is like this?
By this.
END;

TAO_55 :: #string END
Whoever holds virtue deeply
is like a newborn baby.
Wasps and scorpions, vipers and snakes
do not sting it.
Wild beasts do not pounce on it.
Birds of prey do not strike it.
Its bones are tender, its sinews soft,
yet its little fist holds tight.
It knows nothing of man and woman joining,
yet its body stirs:
this is life at its fullest.
It cries all day
and never grows hoarse:
this is harmony at its height.

To know harmony is to know the constant.
To know the constant is to see clearly.
Straining to add to life brings bad luck.
When the heart drives the breath, that is force.
Whatever reaches its peak grows old.
This is called going against the Tao,
and what goes against the Tao
ends early.
END;

TAO_56 :: #string END
Those who know do not speak of it.
Those who speak of it do not know.

Block the openings,
shut the doors,
wear down the sharp edges,
unravel the tangles,
mute the light,
join the dust.
This is called the deep oneness.

Then no one can draw you close
and no one can push you away.
No one can help you
and no one can harm you.
No one can raise you up
and no one can bring you low.
So you become the treasure of the world.
END;

TAO_57 :: #string END
Rule a country by what is straight.
Fight a war by surprise.
Win the world by having no business with it.
How do I know this is so?
By this:

The more taboos in the world,
the poorer the people.
The more sharp tools the people have,
the darker the country’s confusion.
The more cunning skills,
the more strange things spring up.
The more laws are posted,
the more thieves and robbers.

So the sage says:
I do nothing,
and the people change of themselves.
I love stillness,
and the people set themselves right.
I have no business with them,
and the people grow rich of themselves.
I want nothing,
and the people become plain as uncarved wood.
END;

TAO_58 :: #string END
When the government is quiet and slow,
the people are simple and good.
When the government is sharp and prying,
the people grow sly.

Bad fortune is what good fortune leans on.
Good fortune is where bad fortune hides.
Who knows where it ends?
There is no fixed point.
The straight turns strange again,
the good turns ill again.
People have been lost in this
for a very long time.

So the sage is square but does not cut,
has edges but does not wound,
is straight but does not overreach,
shines but does not dazzle.
END;

TAO_59 :: #string END
In leading people and serving heaven,
nothing is better than holding back.
Holding back means yielding early.
Yielding early means storing up virtue,
and storing it again.
Store up virtue,
and there is nothing you cannot overcome.
When there is nothing you cannot overcome,
no one knows your limits.
When no one knows your limits,
you can hold a country.
Hold the mother of the country,
and you can last a long time.

This is called sinking the roots deep
and making the stem strong:
the way of long life and lasting sight.
END;

TAO_60 :: #string END
Rule a great country
as you would cook a little fish.

When the Tao watches over the world,
the ghosts lose their power.
It is not that the ghosts lose their power,
but that their power does no harm to people.
It is not that their power does no harm,
but that the sage, too, does no harm.
When neither does harm to the other,
virtue comes home to both.
END;

TAO_61 :: #string END
A great country is like the low reaches of a river,
where all the world’s streams meet.
It is the female of the world.
The female always overcomes the male by stillness,
and in her stillness she lies low.

So a great country, by lying low before a small one,
wins the small one over.
A small country, by lying low before a great one,
wins the great one’s favor.
So one lies low to win,
and the other is low and so wins.

A great country only wants to gather and feed the people.
A small country only wants to join and serve.
For both to have what they want,
the great one should lie low.
END;

TAO_62 :: #string END
The Tao is the hidden inner room of all things,
the treasure of the good,
the shelter of those who are not good.

Fine words can be sold in the market.
Fine deeds can lift a person up.
And those who are not good —
why throw them away?

So when a new emperor is crowned
and the three great ministers are named,
rather than send a jade disc held in both hands
ahead of a team of four horses,
better to sit still and offer this Tao.

Why did the ancients prize this Tao?
Did they not say,
“By it, whoever looks for something gets it,
and whoever has done wrong is spared”?
So it is the treasure of the world.
END;

TAO_63 :: #string END
Let your doing be not-doing,
your work be no work,
your taste be for the tasteless.
Big or small, many or few,
answer hurt with kindness.

Plan for the hard while it is easy.
Work on the great while it is tiny.
Every hard thing in the world
begins as something easy.
Every great thing in the world
begins as something small.
So the sage never takes on anything great,
and that is how the great gets done.

A promise lightly made
is seldom kept.
Think everything easy,
and everything turns hard.
So the sage treats even the easy as hard,
and in the end finds nothing hard at all.
END;

TAO_64 :: #string END
While it is at rest, it is easy to hold.
Before it gives a sign, it is easy to plan for.
The brittle breaks up like spring ice.
The fine is easily blown away.
Work on it before it is there.
Tend it before it is tangled.

A tree too wide to wrap your arms around
grows from a sprout as fine as a hair.
A tower nine stories high
rises from one basket of earth.
A journey of a thousand miles
begins with a single step.

Whoever forces it, spoils it.
Whoever grabs it, loses it.
So the sage does not force and does not spoil,
does not grab and does not lose.
People often spoil their work
just when it is nearly done.
Be as careful at the end as at the beginning,
and nothing will be spoiled.

So the sage wants what others do not want
and sets no value on rare goods,
learns what others do not learn
and turns back to what everyone passed by,
to help all things be as they are by nature,
never daring to force them.
END;

TAO_65 :: #string END
Those of old who were good at the Tao
did not make the people sharp
but kept them simple.
People are hard to lead
when they have too much cunning.
So to rule a country by cunning
is to rob it,
and to rule it without cunning
is to bless it.

Know these two,
and you have a measure.
Always to know this measure
is called the deep virtue.
Deep virtue is deep, and reaches far.
It turns back with all things,
and so comes at last to the great accord.
END;

TAO_66 :: #string END
Rivers and seas are kings of a hundred valleys
because they know how to lie low.
That is why they are kings of a hundred valleys.

So to stand above the people,
speak as one below them.
To go before the people,
walk behind them.

Then the sage stands above
and the people feel no weight,
goes ahead
and the people feel no harm.
The whole world gladly lifts the sage up
and never grows tired.
Because the sage does not contend,
no one in the world can contend with the sage.
END;

TAO_67 :: #string END
Everyone says my Tao is great
and yet resembles nothing.
It is because it is great
that it resembles nothing.
If it resembled anything,
it would long ago have grown small.

I have three treasures
that I hold and keep.
The first is gentleness.
The second is thrift.
The third is not daring to be first in the world.

Gentle, you can be brave.
Thrifty, you can be generous.
Not daring to be first,
you can lead all who serve.

Now people give up gentleness and try to be brave,
give up thrift and try to be generous,
give up the last place and try to be first.
This is death.

Gentleness wins when it fights
and holds firm when it guards.
When heaven means to save someone,
it guards them with gentleness.
END;

TAO_68 :: #string END
A good soldier is not warlike.
A good fighter is not angry.
One who is good at beating an enemy
does not meet it head on.
One who is good at using people
puts themselves below them.

This is called the virtue of not contending.
This is called the strength of using others well.
This is called matching heaven,
the highest way of old.
END;

TAO_69 :: #string END
Soldiers have a saying:
“I dare not be the host;
I would rather be the guest.
I dare not move forward an inch;
I would rather fall back a foot.”

This is called moving in no ranks,
raising no arm,
facing no enemy,
holding no weapon.

No disaster is greater
than taking an enemy lightly.
Take an enemy lightly,
and I nearly lose my treasures.
So when armies clash,
the side that grieves will win.
END;

TAO_70 :: #string END
My words are very easy to know
and very easy to live.
Yet no one in the world can know them,
and no one can live them.

My words have a root.
My deeds have a ruler.
It is because people do not know this
that they do not know me.
Those who know me are few,
and those who follow me are precious.

So the sage wears rough cloth
and carries jade inside it.
END;

TAO_71 :: #string END
To know that you do not know is best.
Not to know, and think you know, is an illness.
Only by being sick of this illness
can you be free of it.
The sage is not ill,
being sick of the illness,
and so is not ill.
END;

TAO_72 :: #string END
When people no longer fear power,
a greater terror is on its way.

Do not cramp the places where they live.
Do not crush the ways they make a living.
Only if you do not crush them
will they not tire of you.

So the sage knows the self
but does not show it off,
loves the self
but does not prize it high,
letting go of that
and taking this.
END;

TAO_73 :: #string END
Brave in daring, you are killed.
Brave in not daring, you live.
Of these two,
one helps and one harms.
What heaven hates —
who knows why?
Even the sage finds this hard.

The way of heaven
does not contend, yet is good at winning,
does not speak, yet is good at answering,
is not called, yet comes of itself,
is unhurried, yet is good at planning.

Heaven’s net is vast.
Its mesh is coarse,
yet it misses nothing.
END;

TAO_74 :: #string END
If the people do not fear death,
why frighten them with death?
Even if the people always feared death,
and I could seize and kill the wrongdoers,
who would dare?

There is always a keeper of death who kills.
To kill in that keeper’s place
is like hewing wood in place of the great carpenter.
Whoever hews in the great carpenter’s place
will rarely escape cutting their own hands.
END;

TAO_75 :: #string END
The people are hungry
because those above them eat up too much in taxes.
That is why they are hungry.
The people are hard to govern
because those above them are always meddling.
That is why they are hard to govern.
The people make light of death
because those above them grasp so hard at life.
That is why they make light of death.

Those who do not make a business of living
are wiser than those who set great store by life.
END;

TAO_76 :: #string END
When we are born, we are soft and weak.
When we die, we are hard and stiff.
Grass and trees, while they live, are soft and tender.
When they die, they are dry and withered.

So the hard and stiff
walk with death.
The soft and weak
walk with life.

So an army that is too strong will not win.
A tree that is too strong meets the axe.
The hard and mighty are brought low.
The soft and weak rise above.
END;

TAO_77 :: #string END
The way of heaven —
is it not like stringing a bow?
What is high is pressed down.
What is low is lifted up.
Where there is too much, it takes away.
Where there is too little, it gives.

The way of heaven
takes from what has too much
and gives to what has too little.
The way of people is not so:
they take from those who have too little
to give to those who have too much.

Who can take what they have to spare
and give it to the world?
Only someone who has the Tao.
So the sage does the work and asks nothing for it,
finishes and does not settle in it,
and has no wish to be seen as worthy.
END;

TAO_78 :: #string END
Nothing in the world
is softer or weaker than water,
yet for wearing down the hard and strong
nothing can beat it,
and nothing can take its place.

The weak overcome the strong.
The soft overcome the hard.
Everyone in the world knows this,
yet no one lives by it.

So the sage says:
Whoever bears the country’s dirt
is lord of its altars of soil and grain.
Whoever bears the country’s sorrows
is king of all under heaven.

Straight words seem upside down.
END;

TAO_79 :: #string END
Settle a great quarrel,
and some resentment is sure to remain.
How can this be called good?

So the sage holds the tally of what is owed
and presses no one to pay.
Those with virtue keep the tally.
Those without virtue collect the dues.

The way of heaven has no favorites.
It is always with the good.
END;

TAO_80 :: #string END
A small country with few people.
Let there be tools that do the work of ten or a hundred,
and let them lie unused.
Let the people take death seriously
and not wander far from home.
Though they have boats and carts,
they have nowhere to ride.
Though they have armor and weapons,
they have no reason to show them.

Let the people go back to knotting cords to keep count.
Let them find their food sweet,
their clothes beautiful,
their homes peaceful,
their ways a joy.

The next country may be so near
that each can see the other
and hear the other’s hens and dogs,
yet the people grow old and die
without ever going back and forth.
END;

TAO_81 :: #string END
Honest words are not pretty.
Pretty words are not honest.
The good do not argue their case.
Those who argue their case are not good.
Those who know do not know everything.
Those who know everything do not know.

The sage stores nothing up.
The more the sage does for others,
the more the sage has.
The more the sage gives to others,
the more there is.

The way of heaven
is to help and not to harm.
The way of the sage
is to act and not contend.
END;
